Subject: LOD scores and map extension
Message-ID: <ZsnMkvsNmII.r4jkym7HceIJ@rqtl-disc.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/rqtl-disc/c/ZsnMkvsNmII/m/r4jkym7HceIJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

I am doing QTL analysis with an introgression strain, meaning that all recombination breakpoints are clustered in particular areas of the chromosome. This results in considerable map extension, where markers from the introgressed region clump together and those in the native region clump together.  When plotting the results of scanone, this also results in an uptick in the LOD curve over the large gap areas. This doesn't occur in other programs like QTLCartographer (which has its own problems, I'd rather not use it). Is there a reason for this? Should I just try and get around it by imposing map distances on the markers?  It is mostly an aesthetic problem, getting the plots into a publishable condition is difficult when they look rather misleading.  Thanks!

